{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AtpVMe7gWFfeHg1ee9fA8T","type":"tool_result","content":"1\timport { ApiTable } from \"@/components/api-table.tsx\";\n2\timport { CustomizingYourTheme } from \"@/components/content.tsx\";\n3\timport { Example } from \"@/components/example.tsx\";\n4\timport { Figure } from \"@/components/figure.tsx\";\n5\timport { ResponsiveDesign } from \"@/components/content.tsx\";\n6\t\n7\texport const title = \"content\";\n8\texport const description = \"Utilities for controlling the content of the before and after pseudo-elements.\";\n9\t\n10\t<ApiTable\n11\t  rows={[\n12\t    [\"content-[<value>]\", \"content: <value>;\"],\n13\t    [\"content-(<custom-property>)\", \"content: var(<custom-property>);\"],\n14\t    [\"content-none\", \"content: none;\"],\n15\t  ]}\n16\t/>\n17\t\n18\t## Examples\n19\t\n20\t### Basic example\n21\t\n22\tUse the `content-[<value>]` syntax, along with the `before` and `after` variants, to set the contents of the `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements:\n23\t\n24\t<Figure>\n25\t\n26\t<Example>\n27\t  {\n28\t    <div className=\"mx-auto w-full max-w-md text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400\">\n29\t      Higher resolution means more than just a better-quality image. With a Retina 6K display,{\" \"}\n30\t      <a\n31\t        href=\"https://www.apple.com/pro-display-xdr/\"\n32\t        className=\"font-medium text-blue-600 after:text-sm after:font-bold after:content-['_↗'] dark:text-sky-400\"\n33\t        target=\"_blank\"\n34\t      >\n35\t        Pro Display XDR\n36\t      </a>{\" \"}\n37\t      gives you nearly 40 percent more screen real estate than a 5K display.\n38\t    </div>\n39\t  }\n40\t</Example>\n41\t\n42\t```html\n43\t<!-- [!code classes:after:content-['_↗']] -->\n44\t<!-- prettier-ignore -->\n45\t<p>Higher resolution means more than just a better-quality image. With a\n46\tRetina 6K display, <a class=\"text-blue-600 after:content-['_↗']\" href=\"...\">\n47\tPro Display XDR</a> gives you nearly 40 percent more screen real estate than\n48\ta 5K display.</p>\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\t</Figure>\n52\t\n53\t### Referencing an attribute value\n54\t\n55\tUse the `content-[attr(<name>)]` syntax to reference a value stored in an attribute using the `attr()` CSS function:\n56\t\n57\t<Figure>\n58\t\n59\t<Example>\n60\t  {\n61\t    <p\n62\t      before=\"Hello World\"\n63\t      className=\"text-center font-semibold text-gray-900 before:content-[attr(before)] dark:text-gray-200\"\n64\t    />\n65\t  }\n66\t</Example>\n67\t\n68\t```html\n69\t<!-- [!code classes:before:content-[attr(before)]] -->\n70\t<p before=\"Hello World\" class=\"before:content-[attr(before)] ...\">\n71\t  <!-- ... -->\n72\t</p>\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\t</Figure>\n76\t\n77\t### Using spaces and underscores\n78\t\n79\tSince whitespace denotes the end of a class in HTML, replace any spaces in an arbitrary value with an underscore:\n80\t\n81\t<Figure>\n82\t\n83\t<Example>\n84\t  {<p className=\"text-center font-semibold text-gray-900 before:content-['Hello_World'] dark:text-gray-200\" />}\n85\t</Example>\n86\t\n87\t```html\n88\t<!-- [!code classes:before:content-['Hello_World']] -->\n89\t<p class=\"before:content-['Hello_World'] ...\"></p>\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\t</Figure>\n93\t\n94\tIf you need to include an actual underscore, you can do this by escaping it with a backslash:\n95\t\n96\t<Figure>\n97\t\n98\t<Example>\n99\t  {<p className=\"text-center font-semibold text-gray-900 before:content-['Hello\\_World'] dark:text-gray-200\" />}\n100\t</Example>\n101\t\n102\t```html\n103\t<!-- [!code classes:before:content-['Hello\\_World']] -->\n104\t<p class=\"before:content-['Hello\\_World']\"></p>\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t</Figure>\n108\t\n109\t### Using a CSS variable\n110\t\n111\tUse the <code>content-{'(<custom-property>)'}</code> syntax to control the contents of the `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements using a CSS variable:\n112\t\n113\t```html\n114\t<!-- [!code classes:content-(--my-content)] -->\n115\t<p class=\"content-(--my-content)\"></p>\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\tThis is just a shorthand for <code>content-{'[var(<custom-property>)]'}</code> that adds the `var()` function for you automatically.\n119\t\n120\t### Responsive design\n121\t\n122\t<ResponsiveDesign property=\"content\">\n123\t\n124\t```html\n125\t<!-- [!code classes:md:before:content-['Desktop']] -->\n126\t<p class=\"before:content-['Mobile'] md:before:content-['Desktop'] ...\"></p>\n127\t```\n128\t\n129\t</ResponsiveDesign>\n130\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b04f7c89-943a-4e5c-9006-f0b5a86554a0","uuid":"44719b49-b544-4a47-8c77-30c69d7df44d","timestamp":"2026-09-24T21:22:12.534Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jcarr/.cache/markdownattractor/bench/tailwindcss/src/docs/content.mdx","content":"import { ApiTable } from \"@/components/api-table.tsx\";\nimport { CustomizingYourTheme } from \"@/components/content.tsx\";\nimport { Example } from \"@/components/example.tsx\";\nimport { Figure } from \"@/components/figure.tsx\";\nimport { ResponsiveDesign } from \"@/components/content.tsx\";\n\nexport const title = \"content\";\nexport const description = \"Utilities for controlling the content of the before and after pseudo-elements.\";\n\n<ApiTable\n  rows={[\n    [\"content-[<value>]\", \"content: <value>;\"],\n    [\"content-(<custom-property>)\", \"content: var(<custom-property>);\"],\n    [\"content-none\", \"content: none;\"],\n  ]}\n/>\n\n## Examples\n\n### Basic example\n\nUse the `content-[<value>]` syntax, along with the `before` and `after` variants, to set the contents of the `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements:\n\n<Figure>\n\n<Example>\n  {\n    <div className=\"mx-auto w-full max-w-md text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400\">\n      Higher resolution means more than just a better-quality image. With a Retina 6K display,{\" \"}\n      <a\n        href=\"https://www.apple.com/pro-display-xdr/\"\n        className=\"font-medium text-blue-600 after:text-sm after:font-bold after:content-['_↗'] dark:text-sky-400\"\n        target=\"_blank\"\n      >\n        Pro Display XDR\n      </a>{\" \"}\n      gives you nearly 40 percent more screen real estate than a 5K display.\n    </div>\n  }\n</Example>\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code classes:after:content-['_↗']] -->\n<!-- prettier-ignore -->\n<p>Higher resolution means more than just a better-quality image. With a\nRetina 6K display, <a class=\"text-blue-600 after:content-['_↗']\" href=\"...\">\nPro Display XDR</a> gives you nearly 40 percent more screen real estate than\na 5K display.</p>\n```\n\n</Figure>\n\n### Referencing an attribute value\n\nUse the `content-[attr(<name>)]` syntax to reference a value stored in an attribute using the `attr()` CSS function:\n\n<Figure>\n\n<Example>\n  {\n    <p\n      before=\"Hello World\"\n      className=\"text-center font-semibold text-gray-900 before:content-[attr(before)] dark:text-gray-200\"\n    />\n  }\n</Example>\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code classes:before:content-[attr(before)]] -->\n<p before=\"Hello World\" class=\"before:content-[attr(before)] ...\">\n  <!-- ... -->\n</p>\n```\n\n</Figure>\n\n### Using spaces and underscores\n\nSince whitespace denotes the end of a class in HTML, replace any spaces in an arbitrary value with an underscore:\n\n<Figure>\n\n<Example>\n  {<p className=\"text-center font-semibold text-gray-900 before:content-['Hello_World'] dark:text-gray-200\" />}\n</Example>\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code classes:before:content-['Hello_World']] -->\n<p class=\"before:content-['Hello_World'] ...\"></p>\n```\n\n</Figure>\n\nIf you need to include an actual underscore, you can do this by escaping it with a backslash:\n\n<Figure>\n\n<Example>\n  {<p className=\"text-center font-semibold text-gray-900 before:content-['Hello\\_World'] dark:text-gray-200\" />}\n</Example>\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code classes:before:content-['Hello\\_World']] -->\n<p class=\"before:content-['Hello\\_World']\"></p>\n```\n\n</Figure>\n\n### Using a CSS variable\n\nUse the <code>content-{'(<custom-property>)'}</code> syntax to control the contents of the `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements using a CSS variable:\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code classes:content-(--my-content)] -->\n<p class=\"content-(--my-content)\"></p>\n```\n\nThis is just a shorthand for <code>content-{'[var(<custom-property>)]'}</code> that adds the `var()` function for you automatically.\n\n### Responsive design\n\n<ResponsiveDesign property=\"content\">\n\n```html\n<!-- [!code classes:md:before:content-['Desktop']] -->\n<p class=\"before:content-['Mobile'] md:before:content-['Desktop'] ...\"></p>\n```\n\n</ResponsiveDesign>\n","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":130}}}
